{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"ironfisted-humble-shrew","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nCode-review overlay prompts are guarded by standing eval specs (pattern: `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-tests.eval.yaml` — seeded recall + no-fire diff families, deterministic scorers, validated offline with `rebar prompt eval <prompt-id>`). The concurrency overlay's rubric was designed against a corpus of four real races traced to their introducing commits; without a standing eval, prompt edits can silently regress exactly the recall/precision behavior that corpus established.\n\n## Approach\n\nAuthor `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-concurrency.eval.yaml` mirroring the tests-overlay spec shape (`epochs: 3`, `gate: at_least(2)`, deterministic scorers for valid-findings-shape / recall / no-fire). Dataset entries are MINIMAL DIFF HUNKS distilled from the traced corpus (each with a provenance comment naming the source commit), not full historical replays:\n\n- RECALL R1 — unlocked twin: a `subprocess.run([\"git\", ..., \"commit\"])` write to the shared tracker bypassing the locked `event_append` seam sibling writers use (distilled from 9ad46965bd, `eclectic-spotted-barb`).\n- RECALL R2 — check-then-act TOCTOU: `os.path.getmtime(lock)` → judge stale → `os.remove(lock)` with no identity re-validation (distilled from 56e130ff71, `fine-wisteria-solenodon`).\n- RECALL R3 — fail-open guard (known-hard stretch entry): drift between two reads detected, warning emitted, operation proceeds anyway (distilled from 3938c7ed2d, `greisen-draconian-annelid`). The scorer coverage threshold is set so R3 alone failing does not fail the spec — it is a tracked stretch target, and its known-hard status is stated in a spec comment.\n- NO-FIRE F1 — a write lock ADDED around a previously unlocked merge (distilled from 7853af7698).\n- NO-FIRE F2 — an atomic-write seam conversion of process-local scratch state (distilled from 95f97cbeb6).\n- NO-FIRE F3 — the hard FP control: a DELIBERATE probe-then-skip where the skipped work is explicitly optional and the subsequent operation still takes the real lock (distilled from abbc65adf4).\n\nPositive rationale: seeding from traced production bugs makes the eval a regression oracle for the defect classes that actually occurred here (and their nearest benign neighbors), rather than synthetic strawmen; the distilled-hunk form matches the existing eval-spec format exactly.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/eval_specs/code-review-concurrency.eval.yaml` (new file).\n\n## Testing\n\nThe spec IS the test (LLM behavioral eval per the shared test-design standard §10): pinned model in the spec, 3 epochs, deterministic scorers, a predeclared gate. Oracle: `rebar prompt eval code-review-concurrency` exits 0 with the gate met. The no-fire family is the negative control by construction.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The eval yaml exists with ≥3 recall and ≥3 no-fire dataset entries, each carrying a provenance comment naming its source commit; checked by reading the file.\n- [ ] `rebar prompt eval code-review-concurrency` runs and passes its gate in-session; checked by the command's exit code.\n- [ ] The hard FP control (F3, deliberate probe-then-skip) is present and scores as no-finding in the passing run; checked in the eval output.\n- [ ] R3 is present, marked as the known-hard stretch entry in a spec comment, and the coverage threshold is set so R3 alone failing does not fail the gate; checked by reading the spec and the passing run.","id":"c134-2158-ea8a-49f1","parent_id":"292f-57d1-e554-486f","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Standing eval spec for the concurrency overlay from the traced bug corpus"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787250761701844001,"uuid":"f4bfe833-9a0c-4933-b576-559ad14973e0"}